irst the agents commit to a  strategy  then nature determines the state and  agents are assigned types independently  at random according to the state  then agents observe their context and  apply their strategy to choose an action  so the solution concept we're interested  in is an  equilibrium and we say that in  equilibrium agents each maximize their  ex-ante expected payoff given the  strategies of the other agents  now for this work we're primarily  interested in finding the largest revolt  supported in any equilibrium and such  revolts are supported by symmetric  equilibria  in which each agent adopts the same  strategy and in particular the strategy  that they adopt  are the natural strategies that we  described on the previous slide  so it's helpful to illustrate the model  with a motivating example so suppose  that g  is a grid of n agents embedded on a  taurus where each agent has exactly four  neighbors and there's no boundary  now here we can say there are only two  types of agents the new type  agents whose natural strategy is to  never revolt  and the chi type agents whose natural  strategy is to conditionally revolt  according to the threshold where p  equals two-fifths and mu equals one-half  we'll say there are two equally likely  states a the anti-government state  where agents of type chi are more likely  and b a pro-government state where  agents of type chi are less likely  and lastly i'll introduce a definition  that we'll motivate later  and we'll say that candidate agents are  agents of type chi  with at least two kai type neighbors  now in the paper we're able to work  through the example which leads us to  the following proposition  which is that when g is sufficiently  large the event that at least half of  the agents are candidate agents is  common two-fifths one-half belief  that supports a revolt of size one-half  whenever it occurs  uh which helps us build intuition  for y common two fifths one half belief  undergirds a revolt of size one half  now we don't have time to work through  all the details of that example here  uh but we can go through the intuition  so the intuition is that  reasoning about the state serves as a  proxy for reasoning about the size of  the revolt  because the agents don't have any  information about the types of agents  outside of their contexts  beyond the what they believe to be the  state  so in particular agents can use their  context and the prior  to compute the probability that the  state equals a given their context  agents that assign a sufficiently high  probability to state a are candidates  for wanting to revolt  because there are lots of type thai  agents  given that the state is a most likely  and there is unlikely to be very many  type chi agents when the state is b so  that's where we come up with the term  candidate agents  now whether or not candidate agents will  want to revolt even though they're  candidates to revolt no matter what  it depends on how likely it is that the  number of candidate agents will differ  significantly from its expectation  and that leads us to our key challenge  which is that the contexts are  correlated so we can't apply a standard  turn off type down that we'd like to  uh to say that the number of candidate  agents won't differ significantly from  its expectation  with high probability  so here's an illustration of why the  contexts are correlated you can see that  these four chi type agents on the grid  all share one neighbor who i denoted as  a new type agent  in the middle and therefore the status  of whether or not each of those chi type  agents as a candidate  is not independent because their  contexts are not independent  so given the intuition that we were able  to build in the motivating example  the next step is to see how general can  we make the setting  such that our intuition still holds uh  and that  takes up a major portion of the work  that we do in the paper so here we move  into an algorithmic perspective  where we generalize the setting so now  we can return to the concept of three  types of agents the new types the alpha  types and the chi types  with a threshold pair p and mu so we  won't give them a specific threshold  pair now  we'll have more general p and mu we'll  keep the notion that there are two  states  and a distribution over the states and  we'll say a  is the anti-government state and b is  the pro-government state both with their  associated distribution over types  and uh to be a little bit imprecise in  the presentation  we'll say that these labels will assume  that the states are labeled correctly  and that corresponds to the notion that  the the size of the revolt  should be bigger in state a than it  would be in state b  because state a is the anti-government  state  now the agents will have a common prior  p which contains  uh p and mu the parameters of the chi  type agents as well as all the  distributions  and a natural algorithmic question to  ask is given values q star and mu star  is it feasible to efficiently determine  whether a revolt of size mu star is  supported with probability at least q  star  so we can conceptualize that problem as  a sort of standard decision problem  that we call revolt that i've written on  the slide here  and it turns out we won't be able to  solve this problem exactly  as we show in the paper revolt is  np-hard  but we can relax it to a more tractable  problem  in order to do that we'll introduce two  error terms epsilon and delta greater  than zero which will be given constants  and two additional priors p minus which  has p minus delta and mu minus epsilon  and all the distributions  and p plus which has p plus delta and mu  plus epsilon with all distributions  with those definitions we can now  introduce the problem promise revolt  uh which has a graph g  the prior p a value mu star and then  error terms epsilon and delta given as  input  and then the promise of promise revolt  is that  exactly one of the three cases which  correspond to the three output symbols  is true and given that promise  the task is to output the one case that  is true  so we have the three cases omega which  says that a revolt of size mu star minus  epsilon is supported with probability q  star at least one minus delta under the  prior p minus  a which says a revolt of size mu star is  supported with probability  q star within delta of the probability  the state equals a under the prior p  and empty set which is states a revolt  of size mu star plus epsilon is  supported with probability q star  at most delta under the prior p plus  so i think it's helpful to understand  these three cases  with this illustration shown on the  slide  and the idea is that given some u star  a revolt of size at least mu star is  either  very likely to occur in both states of  the world  uh which is denoted by case omega  or it's very likely to occur in state a  but unlikely to occur in state b  which is denoted by case a and lastly  it could be unlikely to occur in either  state  which is denoted by a case empty set  and as you can see in the figure there's  sort of fuzzy boundary regions between  the cases  uh where more than one of the cases  might be true  uh but remember that we are promised  that exactly one of the cases is true  uh and so we only have to output that  one symbol  now briefly to return to our idea of  factional belief from the beginning of  the talk  uh the way to relate revolt and promise  revolt to factional belief  is is the idea we saw in the motivating  example which is that commonp  new belief undergirds revolts involving  high type agents  so it's a little bit more complicated  than it is in the motivating example  because of the presence of the alpha  type agents but it's the same principle  okay so now that we've set up the main  ideas we can go  over a brief summary of our results and  first  the theorem shown on the slide is our  main technical result  which says that we can solve promise  revolt in polynomial time  when the largest degree of any vertex is  limited  and where delta is equal to delta n  which is a function that quickly goes to  zero so as the graph grows  the error term with respect to the  probability of  the revolt occurring  goes to zero quickly  and a surprising corollary to our  analysis to prove this theorem is that  to compute promise revolt in polynomial  time  we only require the degree sequence of  the graph g  uh not the actual network structure of  the graph  in the sense of which vertices are  connected to which other vertices  just what is the degree of each vertex  and lastly we're able to use our  intuition like i said that we built in  the motivating example and applied to  our  uh fundamental case we can actually  extend that intuition a bit further into  some important extensions of the  fundamental case  uh the most important is that we're able  to extend our result to general graphs  so you'll recall in our theorem  we limited the maximum degree of any  vertex  with some more careful analysis we're  able to get rid of that limitation  we can also consider the smallest  supported revolt in any equilibrium  whereas typically we're concerned  finding the largest supported revolt  and lastly we can extend the set of  states to be larger than just state a  and state b that we have in the  fundamental case  now for future work uh the main  direction for future work that we're  excited about is to apply factional  belief as a tool to understand  strategic coordination and cooperation  especially in network settings  and the most obvious place that this  might apply is  in uh relation to social norms in social  networks  because social norms naturally have this  recursive reasoning that's very  reminiscent of common knowledge common  belief and factional belief  uh where when i'm thinking about norms  i'm thinking about  what my friends think about me uh which  is exactly sort of the sort of recursive  rationality uh that that relates to  those concepts  there are also some technical open  questions that we left open  uh one of which is is it possible to  to describe an event or some sort of  practical event  uh that is common p new belief but which  the infinite hierarchy  refers to a different mu fraction of  agents at some step in the hierarchy  so the definition doesn't obviously  require that  each mu fraction of agents at each step  in the hierarchy is the same new  fraction of agents  but it's not clear whether that should  always be the case  uh in a more practical sense  and also as i mentioned earlier when i  introduced the notion of factional  belief  the idea of some new fraction of agents  isn't necessarily the only definition of  a faction  that might apply so there's a question  of whether or not we can use a more  general definition of fractional belief  where we're still able to keep the nice  mathematical properties of common p  new belief but maybe apply it to a more  diverse class of agents and  more sophisticated settings  so lastly i'd like to point you to  the full version of the paper which is  available on archive where you can find  all the details that we had to skip over  in this talk  and thank you for watching
 

